WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.720
[Music]

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:22.920
[Boys are playing basketball, shouting and running.]

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.140
[A whistle blows.]

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:33.420
[A boy on the sidelines with braces on his legs retrieves a stray basketball and throws it back on court.]

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.800
[Narrator:] The  chances are you've never seen this boy before,

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.760
but you've seen many like him.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.149
His name is Harris, Johnny Harris,

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:42.420
and he's in the seventh grade at District
Two.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.340
Though Johnny himself is a stranger to you,

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.030
the disease that made him a cripple is no stranger.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:54.090
It was poliomyelitis, a communicable disease.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:01:07.780
[Music]

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.840
Encephalitis,

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.980
Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever,

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.400
ringworm,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.360
infectious diarrhea,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.960
murine typhus,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.420
malaria,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.180
influenza.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:39.079
Communicable diseases: where do they come
from and how do they spread?

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:42.469
Many diseases like tuberculosis, poliomyelitis,

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:45.430
and influenza survive in man,

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.630
and they're spread from person to person.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.460
Some diseases spread by other means:

00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:53.630
Rats may carry typhus.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.399
Mice may cause bacillary dysentery.

00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:00.130
Mosquitoes bring malaria, dengue, encephalitis.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.930
Ticks bring Rocky Mountain fever, relapsing
fever.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.980
Flies carry typhoid, trachoma.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.149
Fleas carry bubonic plague.

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:13.610
Some diseases spread from contaminated meat
or food.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.540
Raw milk may cause diphtheria, undulant fever.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.080
Polluted water: schistosomiasis, cholera.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.840
[Music]

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:35.660
[An ambulance pulls up at the hospital and a person on a stretcher is taken out.]

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.460
Every year hundreds of thousands of people

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.150
suffer from communicable diseases,

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:48.070
and many die unnecessarily from preventable
infections.

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:51.110
Communicable diseases recognize no boundaries,

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.250
for they've taken their toll of human life
the world over.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.540
Their control may be a national problem

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.330
or it may be regional, involving several states,

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.670
or it may be local.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:09.660
An epidemic might start anywhere and reach
into many states.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:15.110
The classic example is the 1918 influenza
pandemic,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.660
which was first recognized in Boston,

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:22.200
and within a month spread throughout the entire
country.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.310
Today, always watchful for epidemics,

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.020
practicing physicians constitute the first
line of defense,

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.660
but the responsibility for the prevention
of communicable diseases

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.870
is vested primarily in the state and local
health departments,

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:44.250
which are assisted upon request by the United
States Public Health Service.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.980
[Speaker sitting at desk:] The Communicable Disease Center, with its headquarters in Atlanta, Georgia,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:55.480
serves the nation in field investigation,
prevention, and control of communicable diseases.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.190
In cooperation with local health agencies
all over the country,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.080
the Communicable Disease Center, better known
as CDC,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.510
has established diagnostic laboratories, applied
research projects,

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.200
control operations, and field training centers.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.680
CDC employs a variety of professional specialists

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.340
skilled in every aspect of communicable diseases.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.160
[Narrator:] Medical epidemiologists,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.120
veterinary epidemiologists,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.260
public health nurses,

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.060
laboratory technicians,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.440
parasitologists,

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:35.389
entomologists,

00:04:35.389 --> 00:04:38.109
sanitary engineers,

00:04:38.109 --> 00:04:40.569
statisticians,

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.500
training specialists,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.569
and many others who work together to solve
the practical problems

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:51.520
and get results in the fight against communicable
diseases.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.539
It may be a problem concerning a section of
the country

00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:57.240
where year in and year out its rivers rise

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.560
to flood out whole towns and villages.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.439
The flood subsides, leaving in its wake billions
upon billions of mosquito larvae,

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:11.879
and the threat of malaria becomes obvious
once more.

00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:15.819
Scientific information on the subject is discussed
and analyzed,

00:05:15.819 --> 00:05:20.419
decisions are made, and one of these is to
design more effective equipment

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.629
to combat the dreaded mosquito.

00:05:23.629 --> 00:05:26.139
Ground equipment or air equipment,

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:29.319
inexpensive to build, cheap to operate,

00:05:29.319 --> 00:05:31.439
but effective, and its effectiveness

00:05:31.439 --> 00:05:33.479
will depend on many factors,

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:36.569
all very familiar to the engineering mind.

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:39.340
The type of larvicide best suited to the problem

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.650
and how many gallons per minute will the unit
deliver.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:46.120
How large the nozzle, how wide the orifice,

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.180
how much pressure for maximum effect?

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.469
Field test: how well does it perform under
actual conditions,

00:05:54.469 --> 00:05:57.909
gallons per acre, distribution,

00:05:57.909 --> 00:06:03.919
widths of the swath,

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:06.669
size of the droplets.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:16.599
So, it's the job of CDC to develop, experimentally,
procedures, materials, equipment

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:22.469
and to test them until their effectiveness
is established beyond question.

00:06:22.469 --> 00:06:24.749
The proven techniques are then demonstrated

00:06:24.749 --> 00:06:26.699
to the state health authorities,

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:29.729
whose personnel are trained to carry out the
programs.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.660
[Speaker sitting at desk:] In common with
medical organizations all over the world,

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:40.069
CDC is trying to solve the riddle of poliomyelitis,

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:44.889
which kills or paralyzes thousands of people
every year.

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:51.680
[A young girl is making her way across the grass using crutches.]

00:06:52.629 --> 00:06:56.049
[Narrator:] In the research laboratories,
it has been found

00:06:56.049 --> 00:06:59.879
that where flies have access to the feces
of infected persons,

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:02.840
the presence of the virus of poliomyelitis

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:05.050
can be demonstrated in the flies

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.809
by injection into susceptible animals.

00:07:08.809 --> 00:07:13.159
But are flies an important and frequent mode
of transmission?

00:07:13.159 --> 00:07:17.949
Would fly control reduce the incidence of
poliomyelitis?

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.720
[Speaker at desk:] We know now that flies
do transmit another disease

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:32.930
which is a very real public health problem,
dysentery.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:37.499
Today, CDC has organized with state and local
health departments

00:07:37.499 --> 00:07:40.360
a nationwide fly-control program

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.669
to reduce the incidence of dysentery.

00:07:42.669 --> 00:07:47.340
Yet, at one time flies were only suspected
as carriers.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.680
[Narrator:] It has since been demonstrated
that clinging flies

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.539
exposed to a culture of dysentery organisms

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.819
carry these germs in or on their bodies.

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:00.279
If the fly remained on the culture for only
a few seconds

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:03.719
and was then placed on a sterile medium,

00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:06.809
the medium showed colonies of dysentery organisms

00:08:06.809 --> 00:08:11.120
after a period of incubation.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.710
It was found that the same sort of transfer
occurred

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:17.349
when flies went from feces to food.

00:08:17.349 --> 00:08:20.469
Because these scientific observations strongly
indicated

00:08:20.469 --> 00:08:24.379
a probable relationship between dysentery
and flies,

00:08:24.379 --> 00:08:28.279
the development of an experimental program
was decided upon.

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:29.020
In this case,

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:35.820
it was undertaken jointly by the National
Institutes of Health and CDC.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.420
As the first step in the program,

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:44.460
epidemiologists selected an insanitary section
of town,

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.530
a section that was known to have a high incidence
of dysentery

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:53.620
and where it was also known that there were
many flies.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.810
Flies would be controlled in one-half of the section,

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:57.490
but not in the other.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:01.130
But to prove that flies were important carriers,

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.650
it would have to be shown that the morbidity
and mortality rates

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:10.940
of the controlled area had been appreciably
reduced.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:13.720
For this experimental control program,

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:18.660
special tools and equipment were designed.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:28.400
[Men are shown building and assembling equipment.]

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:44.760
[Music]

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.440
Fly populations were measured, identified
and collected

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:55.900
for laboratory studies throughout the controlled and uncontrolled areas.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:09.860
[Music]

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:27.640
[Fly collection activities are shown.]

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:30.400
Schoolchildren from both areas were brought to the clinic.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:38.800
Rectal swabs were made so that bacteriologists
could determine the presence of shigella organisms.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:44.080
[Music]

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.760
Epidemiologists determined the amounts of diarrheal morbidity by going

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.920
from house to house through both areas asking housewives

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:58.210
if members of their families had experienced
diarrhea in the last 30 days.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:02.580
Mortality figures were obtained from the county
health officer.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:09.900
In the area to be controlled, inspections
were made to find the fly-breeding areas.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:55.680
[Music]

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.600
Fly-breeding areas were brought under control

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.040
by basic sanitation procedures.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:48.320
[Music]

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:53.460
This experimental program proved that dysentery

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:59.220
could be reduced from one-third to one-half
by fly control.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.480
[Music]

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:13.700
[A report is being typed.]

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:18.040
[Speaker sitting at desk:] When we knew that
dysentery could be reduced by fly control,

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.920
and the expenditure of public funds was justified,

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.720
the next step was to demonstrate to the states

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.350
the proven operational procedures,

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:31.540
and train their personnel in the application
of these practices.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:36.130
Such arrangements are usually made through
CDC regional representatives

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.100
when requests for assistance comes from state
health officers.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:43.890
[State Health Officer:] Sam, I wonder if you
people could help us.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:45.840
We've got ten counties in this state

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.330
where the death and sickness from dysentery,

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.130
especially babies,

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.600
have always been higher than the rest of the state.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:57.050
[Sam:] Yeah, I know most of those counties
are in the low-income areas,

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:02.450
no garbage disposal, bad sanitation, in fact
everything's bad.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.370
[State Health Officer:] Yes.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:11.120
[The state office stands up and walks over to the window.]

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:15.450
You know we've always wanted to get some fly
control going down there,

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:21.040
but we just haven't had anybody that knows
how to organize the program

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:25.350
and see that it's carried out.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:32.200
We don't have any equipment: trucks, bulldozers,
draglines, sprayers.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.560
[Sam:] We can send you some big power sprayers,

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:37.090
but we haven't the money for trucks and draglines

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.660
and things like that right now.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.850
We may be able to give you a little more help
later on.

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:49.000
In the meantime, could you work out a deal
with your highway department

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.810
to use some of their heavy equipment?

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:55.290
[State Health Officer:] I think we ought to
be able to work something out there.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:15:00.840
Now, about that training course in fly control
in Atlanta next week...

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.850
I can't spare any of my people right now,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.200
and it will be a couple of months before I
can get anybody to Atlanta,

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:10.060
and we want to get this thing going.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:13.610
[Sam:] Since you're going to have a big program,

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:17.130
it might be more economical to start the training
program

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:18.880
right here in your state.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.810
I'll see what I can do with the Atlanta people.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:23.400
[State Health Officer:] Oh, that's fine, Sam.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.430
Now, we've got some more training problems.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:29.400
What are we going to do about all of those
technicians

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.820
that require all the special courses?

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:35.110
Now, you've just got to help me, that's all.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.300
[Narrator:] So CDC trained the personnel of
this state

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:41.000
on the proven methods of fly control,

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.070
and enabled them to set up their own program.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.250
Training is an important part of the CDC organization

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.070
and covers such things as water supply,

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:55.940
stream pollution,

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:59.020
sewage,

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:06.740
housing,

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:09.880
laboratory procedures,

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.380
sanitary engineering,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.090
mycotic diseases,

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:16.440
and many others.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:20.390
Training centers are strategically located
throughout the United States

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:23.810
and are attended by state and local health
personnel

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:29.510
and students from many foreign countries.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:33.930
Audiovisual aids for training are planned
and produced by CDC.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:38.610
[Knobs on a film projector are adjusted.]

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:49.980
All types of printed material are also available.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:53.790
Training at all levels is carried on in the
diagnosis,

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:58.930
prevention, and control of communicable diseases.

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:02.970
CDC is one of the task forces of the Public
Health Service,

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:07.589
a reservoir of engineering, scientific, and
medical knowledge

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:16.569
ready to help each state in its fight against
communicable diseases.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:24.959
Today, as yesterday, the challenge continues,

00:17:24.959 --> 00:17:29.379
and opportunity is great for doctors, for
nurses, for scientists,

00:17:29.379 --> 00:17:31.720
and for many others with the ambition

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:36.160
and with the training to accept that challenge.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:47.240
[Music]

